---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `stops     routes to check
5         24
10        362880
15        87178291200
20        121645100408832000`;

const Figure2 = `Depot -> Chen    2 blocks     nearest
Chen  -> Alvarez 6 blocks     nearest of what is left
Alvarez -> Boyd  4 blocks
Boyd  -> Emeka   4 blocks
Emeka -> Diaz    12 blocks    the only one left
Diaz  -> Depot   8 blocks     the trip home

Total: 36 blocks`;

const Figure3 = `Best possible route: 24 blocks

The heuristic gave 36.
Fifty percent worse, found instantly.`;

const Figure4 = `function willItStop(program) {
  // return true if program eventually halts
  // return false if it runs forever
}`;

const Task1 = `function routeCount(stops) {
  let total = 1;

  for (let i = 1; i < stops; i++) {
    total = total * i;
  }

  return total;
}

let sizes = [5, 10, 15, 20];

for (let i = 0; i < sizes.length; i++) {
  let n = sizes[i];
  console.log(n + " stops: " + routeCount(n) + " routes");
}
`;

const Task2 = `let names = ["Depot", "Alvarez", "Boyd", "Chen", "Diaz", "Emeka"];
let xs = [0, 2, 4, 0, 0, 4];
let ys = [0, 6, 4, 2, 8, 0];

function distance(a, b) {
  let dx = xs[a] - xs[b];
  let dy = ys[a] - ys[b];

  if (dx < 0) {
    dx = -dx;
  }

  if (dy < 0) {
    dy = -dy;
  }

  return dx + dy;
}

function nearestRoute() {
  let visited = [0];
  let current = 0;
  let total = 0;

  while (visited.length < names.length) {
    let best = -1;
    let bestDistance = 0;

    for (let i = 0; i < names.length; i++) {
      if (visited.indexOf(i) === -1) {
        let d = distance(current, i);

        if (best === -1 || d < bestDistance) {
          best = i;
          bestDistance = d;
        }
      }
    }

    visited.push(best);
    total = total + bestDistance;
    console.log("  go to " + names[best] + " (" + bestDistance + " blocks)");
    current = best;
  }

  total = total + distance(current, 0);
  console.log("  back to Depot (" + distance(current, 0) + " blocks)");
  console.log("Total: " + total + " blocks");
  return total;
}

nearestRoute();
`;

const Task3 = `// The best possible route for these six stops is 24 blocks.
// Your heuristic found 36.
//
// 1. Look at the printed route. Find the single leg that went wrong,
//    and say in a comment why the heuristic walked into it.
//
// 2. The rule was "always go to the nearest unvisited stop."
//    Write a different rule in a comment. It does not have to be
//    better -- it has to be a rule a computer could follow.
//
// 3. Answer: could you check all 120 routes for six stops by hand?
//    Could you check all of them for twenty stops? What changed?
`;

const ExampleOutput = `  go to Chen (2 blocks)
  go to Alvarez (6 blocks)
  go to Boyd (4 blocks)
  go to Emeka (4 blocks)
  go to Diaz (12 blocks)
  back to Depot (8 blocks)
Total: 36 blocks`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 2.14: Undecidable Problems and Heuristics</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 2.14</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Undecidable Problems and Heuristics</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Optimization Problems Too Big to Solve Exactly</p>
              <p>Heuristics</p>
              <p>Decidable and Undecidable</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>A Delivery Van</h2>
              <p>Six stops. Visit them all, come back to the depot.</p>
              <p class="fragment">What is the shortest route?</p>
              <p class="fragment">Yesterday's word for that: an <b>optimization problem</b>. Best among many.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Easy to state. Try every order and pick the best.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>How Many Orders</h2>
              <Code code={Figure1} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Every Possible Route" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">At a million routes a second, twenty stops takes <b>3,857 years</b>.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>This Is Not a Slow Computer Problem</h2>
              <p>Routes grow by <b>factorial</b>, which is unreasonable time.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Twenty-one stops is twenty times worse than twenty.</p>
              <p class="fragment">No machine you can buy changes that.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>So Stop Insisting on the Best</h2>
              <p>A <b>heuristic</b> produces a solution that is <b>not guaranteed to be optimal</b>,</p>
              <p class="fragment">used when guaranteeing the optimal one is impractical.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Note the word <i>impractical</i>. Not impossible.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>A Very Simple Rule</h2>
              <p>Always drive to the nearest stop you have not visited yet.</p>
              <Code code={Figure2} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Nearest First" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Six decisions instead of 120 routes.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>And How Good Was It</h2>
              <Code code={Figure3} frame="code" lang="txt" title="The Cost of Being Fast" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Every single step took the closest option.</p>
              <p class="fragment">And it still stranded Diaz out at the far edge.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>That Is What a Heuristic Is</h2>
              <p>Greedy at every step, not clever about the end.</p>
              <p class="fragment">You trade a guarantee for an answer you can actually get.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Routing apps, seating charts, packing trucks &mdash; all heuristics.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Now the Harder Category</h2>
              <p>The route problem is <b>solvable</b>. Just too slow.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Some problems have no algorithm at all.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Start With the Easy Half</h2>
              <p>A <b>decidable problem</b> is a decision problem you can always answer with an algorithm.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><i>Is this number even?</i> Correct output, every input, always.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Almost everything you have written this year is one.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Write Me This Procedure</h2>
              <Code code={Figure4} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Does It Stop?" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Given any program, does it eventually finish, or run forever?</p>
              <p class="fragment">Yes or no. <b>That is a decision problem too.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Useful. Every programmer would want it.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>It Cannot Be Written</h2>
              <p>Not "nobody has managed yet."</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Proved impossible in 1936, by Alan Turing.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">This is the <b>halting problem</b>.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Undecidable</h2>
              <p>A problem is <b>undecidable</b> if <b>no algorithm can be constructed that is always capable of providing a correct yes-or-no answer.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Not slow. Not unsolved. <b>Impossible.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Read <i>Always</i> Carefully</h2>
              <p>An undecidable problem can still have <b>instances</b> you can solve.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Some programs obviously stop. Some obviously do not.</p>
              <p class="fragment">What cannot exist is one algorithm that handles <b>every</b> instance.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Three Different Walls</h2>
              <p><b>Reasonable time:</b> polynomial or lower. Just write it.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Unreasonable time:</b> exponential or factorial. Solvable, not at real sizes. Use a heuristic.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Undecidable:</b> no algorithm exists. Some instances, never all.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Do Not Merge the Last Two</h2>
              <p>A heuristic helps with an <b>unreasonable</b> problem.</p>
              <p class="fragment">It does nothing whatsoever for an <b>undecidable</b> one.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Different walls.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Explaining why some solvable problems cannot be solved at real sizes</li>
                <li>Identifying a situation where a heuristic is the appropriate choice</li>
                <li>Telling a decidable problem apart from an undecidable one</li>
                <li>Explaining why an undecidable problem can still have solvable instances</li>
                <li>Telling the three walls apart, given a problem</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Heuristic</dt>
                  <dd>An approach producing a solution that is not guaranteed to be optimal, used when guaranteeing the optimal one is impractical.</dd>
                  <dt>Decidable problem</dt>
                  <dd>A decision problem an algorithm can answer correctly for all inputs.</dd>
                  <dt>Undecidable problem</dt>
                  <dd>One for which no algorithm can be constructed that always gives a correct yes-or-no answer.</dd>
                  <dt>Halting problem</dt>
                  <dd>Deciding whether an arbitrary program eventually stops. Undecidable.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Create a new <b>JavaScript program</b> named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">2-14-routes</b>.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Count the Routes</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Write the code below and run it.</li>
            <li>
              <b>In a comment:</b> at a million routes per second, how long does
              the twenty-stop case take? Show your arithmetic, not just the
              answer.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Routes" class="mt-3" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Take the Nearest One</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Write this into the same file, below Task 1, and run it.</li>
            <li>
              Follow the printed route on paper. Plot the six stops from{" "}
              <code>xs</code> and <code>ys</code> on a grid and draw the path.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Then mark the leg you would not have chosen.</b>
            </li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task2} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Routes" class="mt-3" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Judge the Rule</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Fill in the comment block.</li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task3} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Routes" class="mt-3" />

          <h5 class="mt-4">Challenge (optional)</h5>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Change <code>nearestRoute</code> so it can start anywhere, then run
              it from all six starting points and report the best total it finds.
              This is still a heuristic. Say what it now costs, and whether it
              found the 24.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Task 2 should produce exactly this.</p>

          <Code
            code={ExampleOutput}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            title="Example Output"
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>36 is the right answer for this heuristic.</b> It is also the wrong
            answer for the problem, and knowing the difference is the point of
            the session.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>The heuristic made the locally best choice six times in a row and finished 50% worse than the best route. Explain how both of those can be true at once.</li>
            <li>Name something you use that almost certainly runs on a heuristic. Say what a guaranteed-best answer would have cost.</li>
            <li>A slow problem and an undecidable problem both leave you without an answer. Why is it still worth having two different words for them? Use the word <b>instance</b> in your answer.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
